ratic environment is the main reason this is not rated stronger for the incumbent.", "watch": "A wave of late Democratic outside spending or fresh polling showing the national environment breaking through in the district could narrow the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 65 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Mike Turner (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Mike Turner seeks another term in a Dayton-area district that leans about four points toward the GOP. He holds a large financial edge, with roughly $556,000 on hand to Democrat Kristina Knickerbocker's $125,000, and a Libertarian candidate also appears on the ballot. Eight of nine ratings outlets call the race Likely R or stronger for Turner; only one rates it Lean R.\n\nTwo July polls conducted for Democratic-aligned groups showed Turner ahead by about seven points among likely voters, though both are somewhat dated and come from the challenger's side of the aisle. Prediction markets give Democrats roughly a one-in-six chance of an upset. The main Democratic hope is a favorable national environment, but Turner's incumbency and resources make him a clear favorite to hold the seat.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1787, "margin": -5.46}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1387, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1203, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.155, "margin": -7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1216, "margin": -7.0}]}